# Supply in projects already selling is thinning, sales and prices are standing still

{cqNameEn} of {cqYear} on the Bratislava new-build market brought steady demand, price stagnation and a shrinking number of available flats in the projects already on sale. Sales reached {cqSales} flats against {pqSales} in the previous quarter. The price level {yqStableEn}, showing {yqM2DirEn} of {yqM2PctEn}% year on year. There were {cqSupplyEn} flats on offer.

At the close of the quarter the supply on the new-build market consisted, according to Residata's data, of {cqSupplyEn} flats across {cqProjects} projects. In the {spProjects} projects on sale from the start of the quarter to its end, the number of available flats {spDirEn} from {spThenEn} to {spNowEn}, that is by {spPctEn}%: {spWhyEn}.

The average price of these flats on offer reached {cqM2} €/m² incl. VAT, which year on year is {yqM2DirEn} of {yqM2PctEn}%. Across the series ({seriesSpan}) the average price has moved within a band best described as stagnation — a year ago the average price was {yqM2} €/m² incl. VAT and a quarter ago {pqM2} €/m² incl. VAT. The price is computed on living area, not on total area including balconies and terraces; for the same flat that yields a higher price per metre than a calculation on the larger base.

The average absolute price of flats on offer stood at {cqPrice} € incl. VAT at an average floor area of {cqAreaEn} m². The average on its own says little about any particular offer, however: {dearestM2RoomEn} flats cost {dearestM2ValueEn} €/m² incl. VAT and {cheapestM2RoomEn} flats {cheapestM2ValueEn} €/m² incl. VAT, a gap of {roomSpreadPctEn}% — more than any movement of the market over the past year. {uShapeEn}

