## The development of supply and sales on the Bratislava new-build market

In all, {cqSales} flats were sold against {pqSales} in the previous quarter and {yqSales} a year ago. Demand is therefore holding at the level it has held all year. This was not a spike caused by one large project: the strongest of them accounted for {qTopSellerShareEn}% of sales. Sales also did not split in the proportions of supply: two-room flats are {r2ShareEn}% of the offer but {r2SoldShareEn}% of sales, while three-room are {r3ShareEn}% of the offer and only {r3SoldShareEn}% of sales.

The average achieved price of sold flats reached {cqSoldM2} €/m² incl. VAT, practically matching the average asking price of {cqM2} €/m² incl. VAT. What sells is therefore roughly what is on offer, not a markedly cheaper edge of the market. It is led by {qD1}, which took {qD1ShareEn}% of all flats sold at an average of {qD1M2} €/m² incl. VAT, and {qD2} with {qD2ShareEn}% at {qD2M2} €/m² incl. VAT — both above the city-wide average for sold flats. The average absolute price of a flat sold was {cqSoldPrice} € incl. VAT at {cqSoldAreaEn} m².

A standing average does not mean developers leave prices alone — quite the opposite. Of the {pdKeptUnits} flats on offer from the start of the quarter to its end, developers repriced {rpUnits}, that is {rpShareOfPanelEn}%. Of those changes {rpUpEn}% were upward, at a median of {rpStepEn}%. Across that whole group it means an average {pdKeptDirEn} of {pdKeptPctEn}%, and it {pdRepriceVerbEn} the city average by {pdRepricePctEn}%. Pulling the other way is the composition of supply: the flats that arrived in the meantime are cheaper ({pdArrivedM2} €/m² incl. VAT) than those that left it ({pdLeftM2} €/m² incl. VAT), which moves the average {pdMixPctEn}% {pdMixWayEn}. The two very nearly cancel — and that is why the average looks as though nothing is happening.

## A view on where the Bratislava new-build market is heading

Tomáš Kamhal, founder of Residata: "Over the past year sales have held at around {avgQuarterSales} flats a quarter. In the projects already on sale, meanwhile, supply is {spThinEn} — by {spPctEn}% this quarter. Whether supply across the market as a whole grows is therefore decided by the new projects entering it.

The first factor is supply. There are {cqSupplyEn} flats on offer across {cqProjects} projects. A stagnant average should not lull anyone. Price lists do move, and they move upward; that the average stands still is a result of what is being added to the offer, not of developers waiting.

The second factor is demand, constrained above all by interest rates and household purchasing power. And what matters here is that a city average tells a buyer nothing about what they will pay: the gap between the dearest and the cheapest layout is {roomSpreadPctEn}% per metre, many times the market's own movement over the year.

Unless interest rates and purchasing power change materially, the new-build market will go on marking time, demand will stay bounded and prices will stagnate. What will be interesting to watch is the development of supply — further growth could already cause a degree of nervousness."

**Author:** Tomáš Kamhal, founder of Residata · tomas@residata.eu
